{
  "configuration_id": "config_008",
  "reason": "Evidence: The strongest signal so far is that multiprofile + scvi_n200 (config_005/006) achieves ~0.65 accuracy, far above singleprofile RNA (~0.52) and all image-only models (~0.21-0.23). Within the multiprofile regime, avgpool and transformer aggregation are essentially equivalent (0.650 vs 0.653). The key untested variable in this high-performing regime is the RNA encoder: we have shown scvi_n200 > pca_n2000 for singleprofile, but scgpt (a distinct generative-model-based encoder) has never been tested. Question: Does scgpt match or exceed scvi_n200 when combined with multiprofile aggregation, and if so, by how much? This isolates the encoder choice while holding profile setting (multiprofile) and aggregation (avgpool, the simpler of the two equivalent options) constant. Why this is the best next experiment: It directly probes whether a different learned representation can push past the current ~0.65 ceiling in the most promising regime, without introducing confounds from modality fusion or a second new variable. If scgpt substantially outperforms scvi_n200, it reshapes the best configuration; if it is comparable, it confirms scvi_n200 as the preferred encoder and lets us move on to testing multimodal fusion with confidence in the RNA backbone."
}

